<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  **Signed variant. Do not edit this file directly.**

  Identical to `Release.entitlements` plus `keychain-access-groups`, which is
  what macOS requires before it will let this sandboxed app use the
  data-protection keychain — where the sign-in session is stored (task 11.38).

  **Why it is a separate file rather than the entitlement simply being added
  to the base one.** An entitlements file containing `keychain-access-groups`
  cannot be built at all without a development signing certificate: Xcode
  refuses with "Runner has entitlements that require signing with a
  development certificate". Adding it to the base file would therefore have
  traded a Mac build that runs but cannot sign in for a Mac build that does
  not compile — worse for everybody who just wants to run the app, and not
  what task 11.38 intended.

  So the base files stay buildable by anyone, and this variant is selected
  only when a signing identity exists, via `TRITRACK_ENTITLEMENTS_SUFFIX` in
  `Configs/Signing.xcconfig`. See `Configs/Signing.xcconfig.example` and
  README.md's "Signing the macOS build".

  `test/platform/macos_keychain_entitlement_test.dart` asserts this file stays
  byte-identical to its base apart from the keychain block, so the pair cannot
  drift.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.personal-information.location</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- Task 11.40. Missing from all four of these files while
	     `com.apple.security.app-sandbox` was true in all four, so a sandboxed
	     macOS build got the file panel and then had no permission to read or
	     write whatever the person picked. Six call sites depend on it:
	     `data_export_screen.dart` (CSV export, backup save, backup restore)
	     and `tag_database_screen.dart` (tag CSV import and export).
	     The keychain entitlement in the `-Signed` pair is the same class,
	     found by task 11.38 the expensive way.

	     Not split into a `-Signed` variant the way keychain access is.
	     The keychain one had to be, because its value is
	     `$(AppIdentifierPrefix)$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)` and a machine with
	     no development certificate cannot resolve the prefix, so the build
	     fails outright — task 11.38 measured that. This entitlement is a bare
	     boolean naming no team and no identifier, so that failure mode does not
	     apply to it. **Reasoned from the difference, not measured on a
	     certificate-less machine** — this checkout has a certificate. If a
	     clean clone ever fails to build macOS because of this line, that
	     reasoning was wrong and it belongs in the `-Signed` pair instead.

	     Put in all four files rather than only the base pair, because
	     `test/platform/macos_keychain_entitlement_test.dart` requires each
	     `-Signed` file to be byte-identical to its base apart from the keychain
	     block.

	     **Declared, not verified.** See `CLAUDE_DOCS/RELEASE_CHECKLIST.md`
	     item H3. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-write</key>
	<true/>
	<key>keychain-access-groups</key>
	<array>
		<string>$(AppIdentifierPrefix)$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
